 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Task Automation Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is task automation?\", \"a\": \"Task automation is software doing a piece of recurring work that a person used to do by hand, started by a trigger and carried out by a fixed rule. Copying a new hire's details into payroll is a typical example. It suits work that repeats on a schedule and needs no judgment.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you build an app to automate your task?\", \"a\": \"Write the task down the way you would hand it to a new colleague: what starts it, and what should happen after. Paste that paragraph into Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder'>AI automation builder</a>, which builds the app and its database. Then test it on last week's real cases before anyone relies on it.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I use AI to automate tasks?\", \"a\": \"Yes, for the steps that involve reading or sorting text, such as naming a document type or pulling a due date out of an email. AI task automation works best when a person still checks whatever the model is unsure about.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the best task automation software?\", \"a\": \"Zapier fits a task that is a chain of steps between apps you already pay for, since a Zap is one trigger followed by one or more actions. n8n fits teams that want to self-host. A task that needs its own screens and data is better built as an app.\"}, {\"q\": \"What do task automation tools do?\", \"a\": \"They start work when something happens or when the clock says so, and they act in another tool according to your rule. Look for task automation tools that keep a history of each run, because that history is where you look when a task fails to fire.\"}, {\"q\": \"Which repetitive tasks should you automate first?\", \"a\": \"Count how often each task happens and how long it takes, then pick the biggest one that never needs judgment. A weekly copy and paste between two systems is a strong first pick. Anything that waits on a manager's sign-off belongs in an approval workflow instead.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you automate work tasks without writing code?\", \"a\": \"Describe the work in plain sentences, exceptions included, and let a builder turn them into logic you can read. With Emergent you change a rule later by prompting. Moving a reminder from Friday to Thursday is one sentence, not an edit to a flowchart.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you automate tasks with AI?\", \"a\": \"Give the AI step one narrow job and a short list of allowed answers, for example sorting incoming files into tax forms and everything else. Run it on 50 old examples first and count the misses. Send anything the model is unsure of to a person.\"}, {\"q\": \"How can I automate my daily tasks?\", \"a\": \"Give each daily task a clock trigger and a rule you can write in one sentence. A 9:00 AM run can sort the attachments that arrived overnight, and a 5:00 PM run can send each owner the items still open. When sorting means reading the files first, pair it with <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder/document-automation'>document automation</a>.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is there free task automation software?\", \"a\": \"Emergent is free to start, and the free tier covers building and publishing an app. Before choosing any tool, check what happens when a free plan's monthly run limit is reached, since some pause and others bill extra. Emergent has no task cap.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
}